1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
The technologies that can destroy us.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:05,000
The technologies that can save us.

3
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
It's Armageddon or salvation.

4
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:11,000
From a nuclear nightmare to a nanobot invasion.

5
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,000
From global warming to clean, endless energy.

6
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Now, Doomsday Tech on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,880
The year 2019.

8
00:00:42,060 --> 00:00:45,480
Tensions between the U.S. and Russia are running high.

9
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:49,797
Russia's deteriorating early warning satellites mistake a U.S.

10
00:00:49,798 --> 00:00:52,040
research rocket for an incoming missile.

11
00:00:52,780 --> 00:00:55,880
A panicked Russian command orders a massive strike.

12
00:00:55,881 --> 00:00:59,880
Dozens of missiles are launched at New York and Washington alone.

13
00:00:59,881 --> 00:01:04,880
With the Soviet attack confirmed, the U.S. decides to retaliate in kind.

14
00:01:04,881 --> 00:01:09,880
Launch keys. Keys. On my mark.

15
00:01:09,881 --> 00:01:14,880
Three, two, one, mark. I got missile away.

16
00:01:14,881 --> 00:01:20,880
All told, 7,000 warheads hit their targets in North America, Russia, and Western Europe,

17
00:01:20,881 --> 00:01:25,880
in Western Europe, killing 189 million people in a half hour.

18
00:01:27,580 --> 00:01:29,560
The entire northern half of the planet

19
00:01:29,561 --> 00:01:31,500
is rendered uninhabitable.

20
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:40,260
The view from up here is always a beautiful reminder

21
00:01:40,261 --> 00:01:43,540
of how fragile and rare life is in the universe.

22
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:47,780
And the fact that we can even enjoy this vantage point

23
00:01:47,781 --> 00:01:50,040
is testament to something else,

24
00:01:50,041 --> 00:01:52,640
to the stunning level of technological progress

25
00:01:52,641 --> 00:01:55,180
human beings have achieved in a very short time.

26
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:02,760
There's a dark side to this story of progress,

27
00:02:02,761 --> 00:02:05,860
for we've reached a pivotal moment in human history

28
00:02:05,861 --> 00:02:09,420
when we as a species possess technologies

29
00:02:09,421 --> 00:02:10,820
that could destroy us.

30
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:16,600
We've always lived in a dangerous world,

31
00:02:16,601 --> 00:02:19,880
But the dangers until now have been more dangers from nature.

32
00:02:21,080 --> 00:02:25,160
Floods, pestilence, earthquakes, epidemics, etc.

33
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:27,820
What has changed is that for the first time,

34
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:31,020
the dangers confronting the world can be of our own making.

35
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:35,120
We are changing the global environment as human beings,

36
00:02:35,121 --> 00:02:39,080
and our technology is possibly running away

37
00:02:39,081 --> 00:02:43,760
to the extent that it can destroy our civilization if mishandled.

38
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:53,220
For 60 years, the ultimate doomsday technology has been and remains nuclear weapons.

39
00:02:54,660 --> 00:02:58,920
Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall.

40
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:02,480
Despite the end of the Cold War more than a decade ago,

41
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:07,160
the U.S. and Russia together have some 20,000 warheads

42
00:03:07,161 --> 00:03:12,320
ready to destroy each other's cities, many still on hair-trigger alert.

43
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:15,800
They're armed, they're targeted, they're fueled.

44
00:03:15,801 --> 00:03:22,080
they will fly out of their silo as soon as they get two very short computer signals.

45
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:23,100
Launch key, sergeant.

46
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:28,640
The magnitude of this threat stems from one unchanging reality.

47
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:36,340
The tremendous destructive power of a nuclear bomb.

48
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:45,300
When a nuclear weapon explodes, it releases unimaginable amounts of energy

49
00:03:45,301 --> 00:03:48,920
literally in a few tens of nanoseconds.

50
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:51,740
That energy is largely radiation,

51
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:56,440
and it couples to the air and materials around it

52
00:03:56,441 --> 00:03:58,960
and drives a blast wave like a big piston,

53
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:01,680
and that blast wave propagates out.

54
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:05,400
In front of that blast wave, there are radiations, heat radiations,

55
00:04:05,860 --> 00:04:10,900
which will, for instance, set buildings afire, set people afire.

56
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:14,960
And then very rapidly, the blast wave will come by and put those fires out.

57
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:19,020
but will crush everything in their path, literally to dust.

58
00:04:20,020 --> 00:04:22,880
By the time the mushroom cloud has formed, everything's been destroyed.

59
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:30,020
If the birth of doomsday technology can be traced to a single moment,

60
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:34,900
it would be a clear summer morning, August 6, 1945,

61
00:04:34,901 --> 00:04:40,600
for the city of Hiroshima and Nagasaki three days later.

62
00:04:41,020 --> 00:04:42,320
It was indeed doomsday.

63
00:04:42,321 --> 00:04:48,680
The bombs dropped over Japan were the only nuclear weapons to be used against humankind.

64
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:51,960
Both bombs were fission devices.

65
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:59,100
The chain reaction at the core of a fission bomb is the splitting apart of the highly unstable elements,

66
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:02,920
uranium-235 or plutonium-239.

67
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:06,460
Though many times more powerful than any weapon previously devised,

68
00:05:06,461 --> 00:05:12,660
the fission bomb was eclipsed a mere six years later by a weapon hundreds of times more powerful

69
00:05:12,661 --> 00:05:15,660
the fusion or hydrogen bomb

70
00:05:15,661 --> 00:05:25,920
a hydrogen bomb also called a thermonuclear bomb uses a primary fission explosion to trigger a

71
00:05:25,921 --> 00:05:33,300
secondary fusion explosion in the fusion explosion deuterium and tritium unstable forms of hydrogen

72
00:05:33,301 --> 00:05:39,400
are fused, turning into helium and giving off extra neutrons in a chain reaction.

73
00:05:40,740 --> 00:05:45,920
Since the Cold War began, the Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists has marked our movements

74
00:05:45,921 --> 00:05:52,040
toward or away from nuclear annihilation by moving the hands of the so-called doomsday clock

75
00:05:52,041 --> 00:05:54,786
closer or farther away from midnight.

76
00:05:54,787 --> 00:05:58,720
We were only three minutes away from midnight in 1953

77
00:05:58,721 --> 00:06:02,160
after the successful U.S. and Soviet H-bomb tests.

78
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:10,300
With the invention of the hydrogen bomb, the evolution of nuclear lethality increased rapidly

79
00:06:10,301 --> 00:06:12,300
and with dizzying variety.

80
00:06:12,301 --> 00:06:18,300
We would have nuclear weapons on bombers that were on airborne alerts circling the globe

81
00:06:18,301 --> 00:06:19,300
24 hours a day.

82
00:06:19,301 --> 00:06:25,300
And when you couple that with the posture that we and then the Russians adopted of launch

83
00:06:25,301 --> 00:06:30,300
on warning, where you wouldn't necessarily wait for a nuclear attack to occur, but you

84
00:06:30,301 --> 00:06:33,080
but you would launch on the warning of such an attack

85
00:06:33,081 --> 00:06:34,780
because you couldn't afford to be wrong.

86
00:06:34,781 --> 00:06:37,700
That just dramatically increased the dangers that we faced.

87
00:06:47,420 --> 00:06:49,680
The introduction of the Intercontinental Ballistic

88
00:06:49,681 --> 00:06:54,920
Missile, or ICBM, was a watershed moment.

89
00:06:54,921 --> 00:06:58,640
An ICBM is a rocket 30 to 100 feet tall,

90
00:06:58,641 --> 00:07:01,880
filled with liquid or solid fuel.

91
00:07:01,881 --> 00:07:05,120
Once it's blasted into space, it can fly at 20 times

92
00:07:05,121 --> 00:07:07,580
the speed of sound.

93
00:07:07,581 --> 00:07:11,060
An inertial, radio, or satellite guidance system

94
00:07:11,061 --> 00:07:13,880
leads it toward the target, and the reentry vehicle

95
00:07:13,881 --> 00:07:15,160
simply plummets to Earth.

96
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:23,920
After ICBMs came supersonic nuclear bombers and submarines

97
00:07:23,921 --> 00:07:27,700
carrying up to 200 warheads, lurking off the enemy's coast

98
00:07:27,701 --> 00:07:30,280
and able to strike at cities in five to seven minutes.

99
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:32,420
Then came MIRS,

100
00:07:33,220 --> 00:07:36,220
or multiple independently targetable re-entry vehicles,

101
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:40,300
missiles carrying up to 10, 300 kiloton warheads,

102
00:07:41,080 --> 00:07:43,660
and small winged nuclear-armed cruise missiles

103
00:07:43,661 --> 00:07:46,060
that could be fired from ships or airplanes.

104
00:07:47,060 --> 00:07:50,460
We had nuclear depth charges and nuclear air-to-air missiles

105
00:07:50,461 --> 00:07:53,180
and nuclear surface-to-air missiles and nuclear landmines,

106
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:55,220
and anything you could put a nuclear bomb on, you did.

107
00:07:55,221 --> 00:08:01,940
Between 1949 and 1990, the Soviets built 55,000 nuclear bombs.

108
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:06,840
During roughly the same time, the United States built 70,000.

109
00:08:07,620 --> 00:08:14,440
With the end of the Cold War in 1991, the doomsday clock moved back to 17 minutes to midnight.

110
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:21,940
But in 2004, it read 7 minutes to midnight, exactly where it was in 1947 when the practice began.

111
00:08:21,941 --> 00:08:30,000
Russia's deteriorating early warning networks and the nature of its nuclear command are among the

112
00:08:30,001 --> 00:08:36,000
reasons for the heightened risk of doomsday Russia today has no effective really early

113
00:08:36,001 --> 00:08:40,360
warning satellite network which means that in conjunction with its decaying early warning

114
00:08:40,361 --> 00:08:45,960
ground-based radar network there are large portions of the day maybe upwards of 15 hours a day where

115
00:08:45,961 --> 00:08:51,400
Russian generals have no idea whether they're under nuclear attack a frightening incident occurred in

116
00:08:51,401 --> 00:08:57,840
1995, when a false reading by Russian early warning satellites caused then-President Boris

117
00:08:57,841 --> 00:09:01,700
Yeltsin to activate his nuclear football for the first time.

118
00:09:01,701 --> 00:09:12,140
And so in January of 1995, a peaceful rocket launch out of Norway toward the North Pole

119
00:09:12,141 --> 00:09:20,140
triggered one of the most serious false alarms in Russian nuclear history, involving, for

120
00:09:20,141 --> 00:09:26,920
the first time to my knowledge ever, a president of either Russia or the United States in a nuclear

121
00:09:26,921 --> 00:09:28,757
decision-making emergency.

122
00:09:28,758 --> 00:09:33,280
The greatest danger we face in the days, weeks, and months ahead

123
00:09:33,281 --> 00:09:39,600
is a nuclear 9-11. We hope and pray that it is not already too late to prevent.

124
00:09:39,601 --> 00:09:40,301
Today,

125
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:46,320
Russia is also struggling to guard its 1,000 tons of highly enriched uranium, or HEU,

126
00:09:46,321 --> 00:09:49,220
and 170 tons of plutonium,

127
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:53,920
enough fissile material to make tens of thousands of nuclear weapons.

128
00:09:54,940 --> 00:09:57,640
The ranking member of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee

129
00:09:57,641 --> 00:10:00,540
has referred to the situation as sort of a nuclear Walmart.

130
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:01,800
You know, if you want to go there,

131
00:10:02,300 --> 00:10:04,660
if you want to get a nuclear weapon, that's the place to go.

132
00:10:05,100 --> 00:10:08,800
There are 18 confirmed cases, mostly in the former Soviet Union,

133
00:10:09,020 --> 00:10:13,060
in which stolen HEU or plutonium has been seized by authorities.

134
00:10:13,061 --> 00:10:24,880
Why would it be a mistake to think that everything is safely protected?

135
00:10:24,881 --> 00:10:35,380
First, because the army itself that is protecting all this is more or less in a state of decay.

136
00:10:35,381 --> 00:10:41,060
It is destitute, and corruption in Russia is thriving.

137
00:10:41,061 --> 00:10:48,060
So it is impossible to rule out that the corrupt army elements could be selling weapons.

138
00:10:52,060 --> 00:10:56,940
Meanwhile, every time another nation goes nuclear, the risk of nuclear war and nuclear

139
00:10:56,941 --> 00:10:59,380
terrorism spikes.

140
00:10:59,381 --> 00:11:04,060
Just as the political situation changed several times in the 20th century, so it may in the

141
00:11:04,061 --> 00:11:05,860
21st.

142
00:11:05,861 --> 00:11:11,700
And we have to be aware that 50 years from now, there could be a new geopolitical situation,

143
00:11:11,701 --> 00:11:17,680
a standoff between some new superpowers, which could lead to a situation at least as dangerous

144
00:11:17,681 --> 00:11:19,640
as the worst period of the Cold War.

145
00:11:19,641 --> 00:11:24,360
The clock over my shoulder is ticking right now, and it's unfortunately, I think, just

146
00:11:24,361 --> 00:11:28,720
a matter of time before the weapons are used if we don't get them under control.

147
00:11:28,721 --> 00:11:33,240
Nuclear weapons, like all technology, cannot be uninvented.

148
00:11:33,241 --> 00:11:39,240
And with great care, they can be maintained, dismantled, and guarded.

149
00:11:39,241 --> 00:11:49,940
Next, technology exists to tame the nuclear dragon and lower the chances of the unthinkable.

150
00:11:49,941 --> 00:11:53,746
In two years, 1959 and 1960, the U.S.

151
00:11:53,747 --> 00:11:58,580
built over 14,000 nuclear warheads, or about 28

152
00:11:58,581 --> 00:12:01,480
warheads, every workday.

153
00:12:01,481 --> 00:12:04,300
Doomsday Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

154
00:12:06,980 --> 00:12:13,100
On the windswept plains of North Texas, inside a sprawling 2 million square foot complex,

155
00:12:13,660 --> 00:12:19,640
the deadliest devices ever designed by man are disassembled very, very carefully.

156
00:12:20,960 --> 00:12:27,040
This is Pantex, now the only Department of Energy site charged with the awesome responsibility

157
00:12:27,041 --> 00:12:29,600
of dismantling U.S. nuclear warheads.

158
00:12:29,601 --> 00:12:33,740
Pantex recently disassembled its 10,000th warhead.

159
00:12:35,040 --> 00:12:38,100
Pantex is the place, the final assembly point,

160
00:12:38,200 --> 00:12:42,260
or initial disassembly point for nuclear warheads in the United States.

161
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:44,840
The nuclear warheads arrive,

162
00:12:45,140 --> 00:12:47,460
moved from the nation's silos and bomb arsenals,

163
00:12:47,740 --> 00:12:48,880
on heavily guarded trucks.

164
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:53,800
In this rare footage, a B-61 nuclear bomb

165
00:12:53,801 --> 00:12:55,640
is separated into its sub-assemblies,

166
00:12:56,680 --> 00:12:58,340
including the radar nose cone,

167
00:12:58,341 --> 00:13:01,620
and the tail section containing the parachute.

168
00:13:02,860 --> 00:13:06,620
Highly valuable electronic instruments are removed and reused if possible.

169
00:13:07,660 --> 00:13:09,900
And precious metals are salvaged.

170
00:13:12,040 --> 00:13:16,480
Pantex workers purposefully disfigure the classified components before disposing of them

171
00:13:16,481 --> 00:13:19,620
so as not to reveal any secrets of the bomb.

172
00:13:20,900 --> 00:13:25,160
Then there is what nuclear weapons engineers call the physics package,

173
00:13:25,161 --> 00:13:29,560
the incredibly complex sub-assemblies that create a nuclear explosion.

174
00:13:30,640 --> 00:13:36,540
The primary, or fission, assembly is built around a hollow sphere of plutonium, called a pit.

175
00:13:37,440 --> 00:13:41,780
In the case of a hydrogen bomb, there is a secondary, or fusion, assembly

176
00:13:41,781 --> 00:13:45,220
built around highly enriched uranium, or HEU.

177
00:13:46,340 --> 00:13:51,020
Dismantling of the nuclear components takes place inside heavily reinforced concrete buildings,

178
00:13:51,021 --> 00:13:52,960
called gravel gerties,

179
00:13:53,560 --> 00:13:56,860
built to minimize the effects of an unlikely nuclear blast.

180
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:03,320
There are at least 12,000 plutonium pits stored at Pantex.

181
00:14:03,660 --> 00:14:08,560
The secondaries are taken intact to the DOE's Y-12 complex in Oak Ridge, Tennessee.

182
00:14:09,480 --> 00:14:15,560
In the U.S., some 58,000 nuclear weapons have been dismantled since the atomic age began.

183
00:14:16,220 --> 00:14:18,260
That's a lot of fissile material to monitor.

184
00:14:18,940 --> 00:14:21,760
They control plutonium down to the level of a gram.

185
00:14:21,960 --> 00:14:24,560
They control uranium to the level of a gram.

186
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:27,860
It makes Fort Knox look like Joe's diner.

187
00:14:29,060 --> 00:14:31,520
You know, we guard this stuff better than we guard gold.

188
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:37,520
And it is accounted, when it is moved from this place on a shelf to another place on a shelf,

189
00:14:37,521 --> 00:14:39,160
that motion is recorded.

190
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:41,020
The U.S.

191
00:14:41,021 --> 00:14:44,740
is also working to perfect the integrity of the nuclear weapons in its arsenal.

192
00:14:44,741 --> 00:14:52,040
This giant gas gun at Lawrence Livermore National Lab blasts a tiny projectile into a sample

193
00:14:52,041 --> 00:14:56,280
of the so-called insensitive high explosive.

194
00:14:56,281 --> 00:15:02,780
This is the non-nuclear explosive placed around the pit in a nuclear weapon.

195
00:15:02,781 --> 00:15:08,180
The weapon casing is enclosed in a steel chamber to contain flying shrapnel should it break

196
00:15:08,181 --> 00:15:14,680
apart on impact.

197
00:15:14,681 --> 00:15:19,300
When the gas gun is activated, the projectile is fired into the insensitive explosive at

198
00:15:19,301 --> 00:15:21,680
18,000 miles per hour.

199
00:15:21,681 --> 00:15:26,920
This test helps the lab to design material so insensitive that it cannot be detonated

200
00:15:26,921 --> 00:15:37,160
by anyone or anything, except by this, the MSAD, or Mechanical Safing and Arming Device.

201
00:15:37,161 --> 00:15:41,760
And in order to get it into line with the gun barrel so that it can set the nuclear

202
00:15:41,761 --> 00:15:48,760
weapon off, you require a very complex series of digits, zeros and ones, a code, which are

203
00:15:48,761 --> 00:15:49,530
mechanical.

204
00:15:49,531 --> 00:15:55,200
And if you don't put in exactly the right series of digits, it locks permanently.

205
00:15:55,201 --> 00:16:00,209
And I just locked it up by putting the wrong first digit in.

206
00:16:00,210 --> 00:16:02,880
And then the weapon is disabled.

207
00:16:03,440 --> 00:16:08,080
You can't set it off. The U.S. Departments of Energy and Defense

208
00:16:08,081 --> 00:16:11,020
are also providing much-needed technical help

209
00:16:11,021 --> 00:16:15,040
to modernize surveillance and security at Russian nuclear sites.

210
00:16:15,580 --> 00:16:18,360
But the main tool in this endeavor is cash.

211
00:16:19,380 --> 00:16:22,200
The DOE is buying large amounts of weapons uranium

212
00:16:22,201 --> 00:16:24,060
from our former Cold War adversary

213
00:16:24,061 --> 00:16:28,140
and blending it into reactor fuel for U.S. nuclear plants.

214
00:16:29,560 --> 00:16:32,040
Ironically, the engine of American capitalism

215
00:16:32,041 --> 00:16:35,660
is running partly on fuel from weapons designed to destroy it.

216
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:42,680
But the post-Cold War downsizing of the Soviets' gigantic nuclear weapons program

217
00:16:42,681 --> 00:16:45,640
left behind another expensive problem.

218
00:16:49,040 --> 00:16:55,180
Over 5 million people were working on research, development and production there,

219
00:16:55,860 --> 00:17:01,160
including over half a million, 540,000 with scientific degrees.

220
00:17:02,040 --> 00:17:02,820
I was one of them.

221
00:17:02,821 --> 00:17:08,100
Three out of four scientists worked for the defense industry.

222
00:17:09,780 --> 00:17:17,080
Many of them worked in closed cities, large, walled-off secret cities that built the bulk of the Soviet nuclear arsenal.

223
00:17:18,640 --> 00:17:27,123
In exchange for their labors and their heavily policed lives, scientists and workers in the closed cities enjoyed a standard of living much higher than that of the

224
00:17:27,124 --> 00:17:28,320
average Soviet citizen.

225
00:17:28,321 --> 00:17:34,560
They could buy themselves refrigerators, cars, and even have separate apartments for the

226
00:17:34,561 --> 00:17:38,000
family, which was a rare thing in those days.

227
00:17:38,001 --> 00:17:43,520
So the main challenge today is to provide good jobs and salaries for these talented

228
00:17:43,521 --> 00:17:49,100
scientists and engineers, to ensure they're not tempted to sell one of the most important

229
00:17:49,101 --> 00:17:54,120
elements of a nuclear weapon, knowledge.

230
00:17:54,121 --> 00:17:59,780
One only hopes that the closed cities problem will be solved without brain drain, that they

231
00:17:59,781 --> 00:18:07,440
will preserve what is in place there and that they will become civilized scientific centers.

232
00:18:07,441 --> 00:18:14,400
If a nuclear weapon does fall into the wrong hands, the next line of defense for preventing

233
00:18:14,401 --> 00:18:18,900
a nuclear 9-11 is detection technology.

234
00:18:18,901 --> 00:18:24,500
This laboratory, for instance, is deployed radiation detection equipment specifically

235
00:18:24,501 --> 00:18:28,500
aimed at detecting materials in a nuclear weapon assembly.

236
